## Introduction

The [MI Social API](https://aplicacoes.mds.gov.br/sagi/servicos/misocial)
(Matriz de Informação Social) is the service behind the public panels of
SAGI, the information office of the Brazilian Ministry of Development and
Social Assistance (MDS). It is a thin HTTP layer over an Apache **Solr**
index, and understanding that shapes everything the package does: what a
document is, why unpublished months come back as zero, and why every
response is checked before it is used.

```{r}
library(misocialr)
```

## Documents: one municipality, one month

Each Solr document holds every published value for one municipality in one
month. A document carries identification fields (`codigo_ibge`, `municipio`,
`anomes_s`, `regiao`...) and one field per published measure. The API has no
data dictionary of its own; `mds_api_fields()` lists, month by month, which
fields exist in the documents of one municipality:

```{r}
fields_recife <- mds_api_fields(2611606)
fields_recife
```

Fields appear and disappear over time (programmes start, are renamed or stop
being published), which is why the result has one row per field x month.

## Field names and storage suffixes

The last token of a field name says how the value is stored in Solr, and
nothing more:

| Suffix | Storage |
|---|---|
| `_i` | integer |
| `_l` | long integer |
| `_f` | float |
| `_d` | double |
| `_s` | string (never a measure) |

`mds_fields()` and `mds_api_fields()` expose it as `storage_type`. The
suffix does not say what the value *measures*: that is the role of the
dictionary.

## The curated dictionary

The package ships a dictionary of 130 fields, grouped in seven themes, each
with a shortcut function:

```{r}
mds_themes()
```

Every entry was checked against the published values. Besides a label and a
unit, the dictionary says the **nature** of each field, which decides what
can be summed:

```{r}
subset(mds_fields("bolsa_familia"),
       select = c(field, nature, nature_basis))
```

| `nature` | Sum across municipalities | Sum across months |
|---|---|---|
| `estoque` (count in the month) | yes | no |
| `fluxo` (value of the month) | yes | yes |
| `fluxo anual` (one value per year) | yes | one value per year |
| `acumulado` (running total) | yes | no |
| `taxa`, `média`, `índice` | no | no |

`nature_basis` says where the classification comes from: `"medido"` means
the monthly series itself was inspected (a running total only grows; a
monthly value oscillates and restarts); the other values are inferences from
a measured field of the same group or from the label and unit.

## Zero is not "missing"

Solr has no notion of "not published yet": a month that was not published
comes back as **0**, indistinguishable at the document level from a real
zero. The package exposes the distinction through facets — counts of
municipalities with a non-zero value per month:

```{r}
periods_pe <- mds_periods("bpc_ben_i", localities = "PE")
tail(periods_pe, 5)
```

A trailing run of months where `n_nonzero` is 0 is the unpublished tail.
`mds_drop_unpublished()` removes it explicitly — and never removes a
non-zero value:

- `reference = "selection"` (default) decides from the localities of the
  query itself, with no extra request;
- `reference = "brazil"` decides from the whole country, which preserves
  legitimate zeros of small selections (a municipality without a CREAS,
  say);
- `action = "flag"` classifies the rows (`publication_status`,
  `publication_reason`, `publication_reference`) instead of removing them.

This is an inference from the published values, not an official publication
date.

## How misocialr talks to the API

Every query goes through the same pipeline:

1. **Validation before any request.** Locality codes (including the IBGE
   check digit), periods and field names are checked locally; a text field
   requested as a measure is an error, not an empty result.

```{r, error = TRUE}
mds_data("bpc_ben_i", periods = 2025, localities = 2611600)
```

2. **Queries by POST.** With many months, fields and municipality codes,
   the URL of a GET would exceed the server limit (HTTP 414), so the query
   goes in the request body.

3. **Paging by `cursorMark`.** Pages are read by the sort key rather than
   by position, so documents added or removed while reading do not shift
   the pages. The package still verifies that the total does not change and
   that no document is read twice.

4. **A response contract.** A partial Solr result, a missing `docs` or
   `facet_counts`, a document without municipality or month, a non-scalar
   or non-finite value, and two different values for the same municipality,
   month and field are all errors with a class
   (`misocialr_error_response`, `misocialr_error_conflict`,
   `misocialr_error_http`, all inheriting from `misocialr_error`) — never
   silently incomplete data.

5. **A per-session cache.** Only responses that pass the contract are
   cached. `refresh = TRUE` re-queries a result,
   `options(misocialr.cache_ttl = )` gives the cache a validity in seconds,
   and `mds_clear_cache()` clears everything.

## How the functions map to the API

| Function | API mechanism |
|---|---|
| `mds_data()`, theme shortcuts | documents, paged by `cursorMark` |
| `mds_api_fields()` | documents of one municipality, field inventory |
| `mds_periods()` | facets: non-zero counts per month |
| `mds_localities()` | documents of one month, one per municipality |
| `mds_drop_unpublished()` | facets (with `reference = "brazil"`) |
| `mds_fields()`, `mds_themes()` | local dictionary, no request |

